# Nginx 504 Gateway Timeout with Docker: The Hidden 60-Second Limit

When debugging a `504 Gateway Time-out` error in a Dockerized PHP environment, it is easy to assume that increasing timeout values in your configuration files will resolve the issue. However, there is often a hardcoded limit deeper in the system stack that overrides these settings, causing requests to fail after exactly 60 seconds regardless of how you configure Nginx or PHP-FPM.

The scenario described involves a reverse proxy setup where Nginx forwards requests to a PHP application running via FastCGI. The symptoms are specific: the request times out precisely at the 60-second mark, and logs from the backend PHP application confirm that the script is still actively executing and writing output. This behavior strongly suggests that the timeout is not occurring within the application logic but rather at the connection layer between Nginx and the PHP-FPM worker process.

### The Default FastCGI Timeout

In standard Linux distributions and many base Docker images, there is a default value for `fastcgi_read_timeout` in the `/etc/nginx/fastcgi_params` file. If your custom configuration attempts to set this timeout but fails to include the corresponding directive in the specific location block or if the default file takes precedence, Nginx will terminate the connection after 60 seconds.

This is a common pitfall when debugging Docker containers because base images often ship with `fastcgi_read_timeout 60;` hardcoded into their default parameter files. Even if your `nginx.conf` includes a global setting of `300` or `600`, you must ensure that the specific location block handling your PHP requests explicitly overrides this default, or that you modify the included `fastcgi_params` file to remove the hard limit.

### Configuration Verification

To resolve this, you need to verify which configuration file is actually being applied. In a Docker container, Nginx typically includes `/etc/nginx/fastcgi_params`. If your custom `nginx.conf` does not explicitly redefine `fastcgi_read_timeout` within the `location` block that serves your PHP application, the global setting might be ignored in favor of the default included parameters, or the global setting might not apply correctly depending on how the `include` directive is structured.

Here is a corrected approach for the Nginx configuration:

```nginx
http {
    # ... other settings ...

    # Explicitly define timeouts in the http block if not already overridden per-location
    fastcgi_read_timeout 300;
    fastcgi_connect_timeout 600;
    fastcgi_send_timeout 600;

    server {
        location /app {
            proxy_pass http://backend;

            # Ensure these are set specifically here if using proxy_pass
            proxy_read_timeout 300;
            proxy_connect_timeout 600;
            proxy_send_timeout 600;
        }

        location ~ \.php$ {
            root /var/www/html;
            fastcgi_pass php-fpm:9000;

            # This is the critical line often missing in default configs
            fastcgi_read_timeout 300; 
            fastcgi_connect_timeout 600;
            fastcgi_send_timeout 600;

            include fastcgi_params;
        }
    }
}
```

Note that `fastcgi_read_timeout` is the directive that controls how long Nginx waits for a response from PHP-FPM. If this is set to the default (or if it remains 60 seconds due to an unmodified default file), increasing `proxy_read_timeout` on the upstream connection will not help, because the FastCGI protocol handshake itself has timed out.

### Docker-Specific Considerations

In a Docker environment, file permissions and volume mounts can sometimes interfere with configuration overrides. If you are mounting your Nginx configuration files from the host into the container, ensure that the mounted `nginx.conf` or `fastcgi_params` files actually contain the updated timeout values and that the container is using those specific files rather than falling back to defaults defined in the base image.

Additionally, verify that the PHP-FPM side is not hitting its own internal limits before Nginx times out. While you have already adjusted `max_execution_time` in `php.ini`, remember that `fastcgi_read_timeout` must be greater than or equal to your application's maximum execution time. If your script takes 45 seconds to run but `fastcgi_read_timeout` is 60, it should work. However, if the default file enforces a strict 60-second cap and your script runs slightly longer, you will see exactly the behavior described in the source material.

Finally, ensure that the `proxy_pass` directive (if used) does not have its own timeout that conflicts with the FastCGI settings. When using `fastcgi_pass`, the relevant directives are `fastcgi_read_timeout`, `fastcgi_connect_timeout`, and `fastcgi_send_timeout`. These must be explicitly set in the location block serving PHP requests to guarantee they override any defaults inherited from included parameter files.
